WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.411
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:03.411 --> 00:00:04.910
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
Welcome, everyone.

00:00:04.910 --> 00:00:07.299
Thanks for coming to
our session, where

00:00:07.299 --> 00:00:09.340
we're going to talk about
the future of meetings.

00:00:09.340 --> 00:00:10.780
My name is Serge Lachapelle.

00:00:10.780 --> 00:00:15.350
I'm a product manager, working
on enterprise collaboration.

00:00:15.350 --> 00:00:18.237
And this is my colleague.

00:00:18.237 --> 00:00:20.320
PATRICK WYNN: Patrick Winn,
also a product manager

00:00:20.320 --> 00:00:24.460
on Hangouts, and specifically
for video meetings.

00:00:24.460 --> 00:00:28.790
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
And so, today I

00:00:28.790 --> 00:00:31.880
want to reinforce some
of the messages you heard

00:00:31.880 --> 00:00:33.990
this morning at the keynote.

00:00:33.990 --> 00:00:37.430
We're all in on
enterprise communication.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:42.650
And whether it is textural
team communication over chat,

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:45.800
team communication over
video, and extending

00:00:45.800 --> 00:00:49.400
into interactions, such as
with the white board or the jam

00:00:49.400 --> 00:00:50.330
board.

00:00:50.330 --> 00:00:54.320
And so, today, this session
will go do a deep dive

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:58.490
on the Hangouts Meet experience
that we presented this morning.

00:01:01.980 --> 00:01:05.820
And I'm going to talk about an
epidemic throughout this talk.

00:01:05.820 --> 00:01:10.490
And it's a silent
epidemic in the workplace.

00:01:10.490 --> 00:01:13.430
More specifically, I'm
talking about the fact

00:01:13.430 --> 00:01:17.765
that 16.7% percent of every
work day is getting wasted.

00:01:20.450 --> 00:01:24.380
My day is full of 30-minute
meetings, and many of them

00:01:24.380 --> 00:01:27.200
are online meetings.

00:01:27.200 --> 00:01:30.710
And imagine losing
five minutes to set up

00:01:30.710 --> 00:01:33.180
for every one of those meetings.

00:01:33.180 --> 00:01:37.790
That's 16.7% percent
of your allocated time.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:40.400
So, of course I use this
number figuratively, but still

00:01:40.400 --> 00:01:41.810
an incredible number.

00:01:41.810 --> 00:01:43.880
Even if it was 5%.

00:01:43.880 --> 00:01:45.980
Imagine being able to give
your workforce and all

00:01:45.980 --> 00:01:48.090
of your teams 5%
of their time back.

00:01:51.502 --> 00:01:52.710
And here's how we waste time.

00:01:55.450 --> 00:01:58.750
We waste time figuring
how to get things to work.

00:01:58.750 --> 00:02:03.250
You have to download software
for many online meeting

00:02:03.250 --> 00:02:05.710
experiences.

00:02:05.710 --> 00:02:09.009
You might not have the
administrative privileges

00:02:09.009 --> 00:02:10.500
to install software.

00:02:10.500 --> 00:02:14.380
Your IT department might
be white listing software.

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:17.110
So you have to go file
a ticket, get permission

00:02:17.110 --> 00:02:22.570
to install piece of software
X to get into a meeting.

00:02:22.570 --> 00:02:25.780
Of course, the usual struggles
with the audio and video

00:02:25.780 --> 00:02:29.590
issues of trying to understand
which microphone you're using,

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.300
the cameras you're using.

00:02:31.300 --> 00:02:33.820
And then, one that
we often forget

00:02:33.820 --> 00:02:37.570
is that, "Oh I'm using
system X, so I'll

00:02:37.570 --> 00:02:39.349
need a system X account".

00:02:39.349 --> 00:02:41.140
And then I'll have to
go create an account,

00:02:41.140 --> 00:02:44.230
I have to remember the
password, I have to log in.

00:02:44.230 --> 00:02:50.780
All of these things cost us
time and make us less efficient.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.040
I work with global teams.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.840
I'm based out of
Stockholm, in Sweden.

00:02:53.840 --> 00:02:56.870
Patrick is based
in the Bay Area.

00:02:56.870 --> 00:02:59.780
Many of my colleagues
here, in front with me,

00:02:59.780 --> 00:03:03.740
work out of our Kirkland, right
outside of Seattle office.

00:03:03.740 --> 00:03:06.570
And we work very
closely together.

00:03:06.570 --> 00:03:09.920
So, we need tools that
can support our reality

00:03:09.920 --> 00:03:12.220
of global engineering.

00:03:12.220 --> 00:03:14.630
We're all on the
move, we all try

00:03:14.630 --> 00:03:18.140
to balance life,
family life, and work.

00:03:18.140 --> 00:03:20.120
Being able to take
meetings from outside

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:23.030
of the office, where on the
go, is an important part

00:03:23.030 --> 00:03:25.610
of our workflow.

00:03:25.610 --> 00:03:29.270
And more and more,
the company intranet

00:03:29.270 --> 00:03:32.090
doesn't mean anything anymore.

00:03:32.090 --> 00:03:34.750
We source out some
engineering, we source

00:03:34.750 --> 00:03:36.380
out some testing,
some marketing,

00:03:36.380 --> 00:03:39.800
you work with legal people
from other companies.

00:03:39.800 --> 00:03:45.260
And so, this domain focus
on communication tools

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:49.490
needs to be broken up because
it doesn't match the workflows

00:03:49.490 --> 00:03:50.300
that we have today.

00:03:53.940 --> 00:03:56.740
So I did some napkin math.

00:03:56.740 --> 00:03:58.680
In my talk, yesterday,
I shared a little bit

00:03:58.680 --> 00:04:01.900
about the meeting numbers
at Google internally.

00:04:01.900 --> 00:04:06.750
We've got somewhere close
to 70,000 employees.

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:11.850
These employees dial in to
video meetings 230,000 times

00:04:11.850 --> 00:04:14.170
every day.

00:04:14.170 --> 00:04:18.320
They dial in across their
laptops, their phones,

00:04:18.320 --> 00:04:20.410
or the meeting rooms.

00:04:20.410 --> 00:04:23.350
And we spend about
nine years, actually

00:04:23.350 --> 00:04:28.490
9.2, 9.3 years of time every
day in a video meeting.

00:04:28.490 --> 00:04:33.910
So, if we were to incur a
loss of a loss of five minutes

00:04:33.910 --> 00:04:37.000
for every one of those meetings,
due to trying to set up

00:04:37.000 --> 00:04:39.100
or trying to get
things going, that's

00:04:39.100 --> 00:04:44.940
over $200,000 in lost
productivity every day.

00:04:44.940 --> 00:04:48.300
I'd like to find
a CIO that would

00:04:48.300 --> 00:04:51.840
be willing to sign a purchase
order for $200,000 every day

00:04:51.840 --> 00:04:54.250
because of lost productivity.

00:04:54.250 --> 00:04:56.040
So, this is a fun number.

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.870
But, really, what
it's all about is

00:04:57.870 --> 00:05:01.530
about removing the interruptions
that can create frustration.

00:05:01.530 --> 00:05:03.480
These break the flow.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:06.690
They hurt our ability
to do good teamwork,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.690
and they harm these
moments of connection

00:05:09.690 --> 00:05:12.540
that you create
with your teams that

00:05:12.540 --> 00:05:14.100
are spread all over the world.

00:05:16.998 --> 00:05:22.430
And, while we can look
at Google's use case,

00:05:22.430 --> 00:05:24.700
you've all been
incredible at giving us

00:05:24.700 --> 00:05:29.830
feedback on the solutions
that we had in the enterprise.

00:05:29.830 --> 00:05:33.480
And what's staggering
is that Patrick and I,

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:37.240
we've talked to many of you that
use video meetings more than

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.620
what Google does.

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:40.210
And you use video
meetings in ways

00:05:40.210 --> 00:05:42.850
that are very different
to what Google does.

00:05:42.850 --> 00:05:46.300
And we've learned
a lot from these.

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.290
If we were to
categorize the feedback,

00:05:50.290 --> 00:05:53.500
we'd put them under
several pillars,

00:05:53.500 --> 00:05:56.350
and I'll walk through
these pillars now.

00:05:56.350 --> 00:05:58.180
Things are too hard to access.

00:05:58.180 --> 00:06:02.080
Hard to get into a meeting, hard
to invite people, especially

00:06:02.080 --> 00:06:06.790
customers, people that are
external to your domain.

00:06:06.790 --> 00:06:09.310
I often use the example,
I want General Motors

00:06:09.310 --> 00:06:12.460
to be able to talk to Ford,
as a hypothetical example.

00:06:12.460 --> 00:06:13.870
And this should work.

00:06:13.870 --> 00:06:16.300
You can expect Ford employees
to have General Motor

00:06:16.300 --> 00:06:17.650
addresses, or vice versa.

00:06:20.820 --> 00:06:24.060
And when things fail,
you want a backup.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:26.520
Maybe you're on the road,
maybe you're driving.

00:06:26.520 --> 00:06:28.530
The ability to
dial into a meeting

00:06:28.530 --> 00:06:32.340
was also a key piece of
feedback that we've received.

00:06:32.340 --> 00:06:34.620
And access is also
about not missing out

00:06:34.620 --> 00:06:37.230
if you can't be there,
if you're not available.

00:06:37.230 --> 00:06:43.476
And recording was a key
piece of feedback in these.

00:06:43.476 --> 00:06:45.270
These systems are hard to use.

00:06:45.270 --> 00:06:47.940
There's a lot of buttons,
there's a lot of knobs.

00:06:47.940 --> 00:06:50.900
It breaks confidence.

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:57.940
Simple actions just as
presenting feel contrived them

00:06:57.940 --> 00:07:02.810
and, again, you lose this
feeling of confidence

00:07:02.810 --> 00:07:06.210
when you're talking
to your audience.

00:07:06.210 --> 00:07:08.000
Another piece of
feedback we've gotten

00:07:08.000 --> 00:07:11.910
is that a lot of the
systems out there.

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:13.950
The user experience
is different,

00:07:13.950 --> 00:07:17.040
whether in your conference
room, on your laptop,

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.550
or on your phone.

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.790
And so, trying to
add consistency

00:07:20.790 --> 00:07:26.290
across all the devices
one supports is important.

00:07:26.290 --> 00:07:28.660
We've heard a lot about
performance issues,

00:07:28.660 --> 00:07:31.090
audio issues, video issues.

00:07:31.090 --> 00:07:33.010
User experiences
that aren't snappy,

00:07:33.010 --> 00:07:35.350
aren't responsive enough.

00:07:35.350 --> 00:07:38.610
Bandwidth requirements
that are too high,

00:07:38.610 --> 00:07:41.430
deployments that don't work
across corporate networks,

00:07:41.430 --> 00:07:48.330
that are firewalled, that
have enterprise requirements.

00:07:48.330 --> 00:07:55.350
And so, of course, we took a
deep look at our own usage.

00:07:55.350 --> 00:08:00.180
But the fantastic thing
was all this feedback

00:08:00.180 --> 00:08:02.220
that we've been able
to receive from you

00:08:02.220 --> 00:08:04.860
and build into a great product.

00:08:04.860 --> 00:08:09.110
And so, that's why today, we're
introducing Hangouts Meet.

00:08:09.110 --> 00:08:12.440
We feel that this is
a videoconferencing

00:08:12.440 --> 00:08:19.020
experience that will give
you 16.7% of your day back.

00:08:19.020 --> 00:08:21.990
And it just works.

00:08:21.990 --> 00:08:24.915
So, we're going to do
a demo, and Patrick

00:08:24.915 --> 00:08:26.970
is going to help out.

00:08:26.970 --> 00:08:31.290
A little bit similar to the
demo that was done this morning,

00:08:31.290 --> 00:08:34.710
but we're going to go much
more in-depth into it.

00:08:34.710 --> 00:08:39.600
So, let's switch to
the laptop, please.

00:08:39.600 --> 00:08:41.669
And so, this is Google Calendar.

00:08:41.669 --> 00:08:46.890
And the Cloudy Coffee Company
has been invited to a meeting.

00:08:46.890 --> 00:08:49.560
And, just as normal
with a calendar invite,

00:08:49.560 --> 00:08:53.380
you can open it and follow
the link to the meeting.

00:08:53.380 --> 00:08:56.430
So, from Google
Calendar, from an email,

00:08:56.430 --> 00:09:01.740
from your favorite messaging
app, that link can be opened.

00:09:01.740 --> 00:09:06.330
This is a Chrome
browser, no plug-in,

00:09:06.330 --> 00:09:11.340
no software was downloaded
to get to this experience.

00:09:11.340 --> 00:09:13.710
And everything I'm
going to demo today,

00:09:13.710 --> 00:09:15.360
no download, no software.

00:09:15.360 --> 00:09:17.670
Native to the browser.

00:09:17.670 --> 00:09:19.240
This is a green room.

00:09:19.240 --> 00:09:22.620
This is where we
can check our hair,

00:09:22.620 --> 00:09:25.780
this is where we
can mute ourselves.

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:27.510
It's to make sure
as well that I don't

00:09:27.510 --> 00:09:30.930
join a meeting by accident,
that I join when I'm ready.

00:09:30.930 --> 00:09:35.010
But, more importantly, if I'm
in a Google-powered conference

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:39.120
room, I might want to not
join the full meeting.

00:09:39.120 --> 00:09:42.090
Maybe I'm in this room just
to present to the meeting

00:09:42.090 --> 00:09:44.460
because I have the video
conference already running

00:09:44.460 --> 00:09:45.750
in front of me.

00:09:45.750 --> 00:09:49.350
And, you know, I took this out
of my little travel bag today.

00:09:49.350 --> 00:09:51.870
These are all the
adapters I travel with.

00:09:51.870 --> 00:09:58.480
HDMI to us USB C, DVI to
display port, VGA, of course,

00:09:58.480 --> 00:10:00.240
because, you know,
you can't go anywhere

00:10:00.240 --> 00:10:03.480
without 1980s technology.

00:10:03.480 --> 00:10:08.227
And this is VGA to USB
C. I mean, this is like--

00:10:08.227 --> 00:10:09.690
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:09.690 --> 00:10:13.630
I don't know about you,
but this feels wrong.

00:10:13.630 --> 00:10:17.190
So, if I want to just
present, I present.

00:10:17.190 --> 00:10:20.410
I choose what I want to present,
and it shows up on the TV

00:10:20.410 --> 00:10:22.950
and to my audience online.

00:10:22.950 --> 00:10:25.170
But now, we're going to
go in the meeting full-in.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:29.490
And before we do that,
this is an i5 Chromebook

00:10:29.490 --> 00:10:35.400
from early last year, and
we're just running Chrome.

00:10:35.400 --> 00:10:38.400
And I want you to take
attention to how quickly I

00:10:38.400 --> 00:10:41.820
get into the meeting from here.

00:10:41.820 --> 00:10:43.960
Boom.

00:10:43.960 --> 00:10:49.270
So, this is about 80, 90% faster
than the classic Hangouts.

00:10:49.270 --> 00:10:50.749
[APPALUSE]

00:10:50.749 --> 00:10:53.710
Thank you.

00:10:53.710 --> 00:10:56.560
And if we're allowed to
geek out a little bit,

00:10:56.560 --> 00:10:58.600
we've rebuilt the
whole JavaScript,

00:10:58.600 --> 00:11:03.010
it uses half the RAM of the
classic Hangouts experience.

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:06.850
This experience renders
much, much more video frames

00:11:06.850 --> 00:11:09.700
per second than the
previous experience.

00:11:09.700 --> 00:11:14.380
And it also reaches HD
quality much faster,

00:11:14.380 --> 00:11:17.050
whereas the classic Hangout
could take up to 10 seconds

00:11:17.050 --> 00:11:21.520
to reach HD quality to ramp up,
to not overload the network.

00:11:21.520 --> 00:11:24.890
This one can do it
well under a second.

00:11:24.890 --> 00:11:28.810
In fact, we're seeing average
times of 100, 200 milliseconds

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:30.850
to reach HD quality.

00:11:30.850 --> 00:11:35.050
So, online with me, we have our
product managers in Kirkland.

00:11:35.050 --> 00:11:36.960
Hi, Erika.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:37.870
ERIKA: Hi.

00:11:37.870 --> 00:11:39.411
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
And we have Boyana,

00:11:39.411 --> 00:11:42.040
who is sitting in a Chromebox
for Meeting-powered meeting

00:11:42.040 --> 00:11:43.300
room in our Seattle office.

00:11:43.300 --> 00:11:43.990
BOYANA: Hello.

00:11:43.990 --> 00:11:48.380
SERGE LACHAPELLE:
Hi, and hi to both.

00:11:48.380 --> 00:11:51.040
So, I'll walk you through
the UI of this experience.

00:11:51.040 --> 00:11:53.470
So we've worked hard at
making things simple,

00:11:53.470 --> 00:11:57.839
getting things out of the
way of your conversation.

00:11:57.839 --> 00:11:59.380
One of the first
things we've learned

00:11:59.380 --> 00:12:03.595
is that when we do
document sharing,

00:12:03.595 --> 00:12:06.340
they are often taller
than they are wide.

00:12:06.340 --> 00:12:10.090
So we've moved the
thumbnails to the side.

00:12:10.090 --> 00:12:13.210
We've also made the
commands to control

00:12:13.210 --> 00:12:14.350
the meeting very simple.

00:12:14.350 --> 00:12:17.710
So, by just moving your mouse,
you see, I can mute myself,

00:12:17.710 --> 00:12:21.190
I can hang up, I
can mute the camera,

00:12:21.190 --> 00:12:24.779
and most importantly,
I can easily present.

00:12:24.779 --> 00:12:26.320
And we've worked
with the Chrome team

00:12:26.320 --> 00:12:30.310
to redo the whole
presentation UI flow.

00:12:30.310 --> 00:12:33.100
So that now, you will
have an experience

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:35.350
that puts you much
more in control of what

00:12:35.350 --> 00:12:36.910
you're selecting to present.

00:12:36.910 --> 00:12:42.010
Whether it's a Chrome tab, a
window, or your whole desktop.

00:12:42.010 --> 00:12:42.820
Thanks, Patrick.

00:12:42.820 --> 00:12:46.840
And, finally, this is a meeting.

00:12:46.840 --> 00:12:49.090
I might want to invite
other people that

00:12:49.090 --> 00:12:52.330
were not on the calendar
to this meeting.

00:12:52.330 --> 00:12:55.330
So, I can easily pull up
the meeting information,

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:59.140
copy this to my clipboard,
and share it out.

00:12:59.140 --> 00:13:02.030
And I think this is what
we're going to do now.

00:13:02.030 --> 00:13:05.890
So, we're going to pretend
that we're virtually something

00:13:05.890 --> 00:13:06.950
that email out.

00:13:06.950 --> 00:13:09.940
And now, we have
someone knocking in.

00:13:09.940 --> 00:13:12.410
Katie works out the
Cloudy Coffee Company,

00:13:12.410 --> 00:13:14.140
she's outside of Hooli.

00:13:14.140 --> 00:13:16.300
She doesn't have
a Gmail address.

00:13:16.300 --> 00:13:19.480
She has never heard of Google,
she's never heard of Google.

00:13:19.480 --> 00:13:22.010
But she can still come
in without any accounts.

00:13:22.010 --> 00:13:25.180
So, let's admit Katie
into our meeting.

00:13:25.180 --> 00:13:28.320
And Katie is somewhere
here sitting.

00:13:28.320 --> 00:13:31.120
Ah, Can you say hi?

00:13:31.120 --> 00:13:38.069
So, she just joined on the
iOS client for Hangouts meet.

00:13:38.069 --> 00:13:39.235
And this was also a problem.

00:13:41.830 --> 00:13:45.460
Individuals that are sometimes
doing freelancing or going

00:13:45.460 --> 00:13:48.730
to an interview, they don't want
to share their private Gmail

00:13:48.730 --> 00:13:50.420
address.

00:13:50.420 --> 00:13:54.760
I remember talking with Chris
about this a while back.

00:13:54.760 --> 00:13:57.850
We've seen people with
Gmail addresses such as,

00:13:57.850 --> 00:14:01.780
IHateBabySeal78@Gmail.com.

00:14:01.780 --> 00:14:04.000
And you don't want to
show that to an employer,

00:14:04.000 --> 00:14:06.700
you don't want to show
that in a meeting context,

00:14:06.700 --> 00:14:09.670
and you don't want to force
people to go create new Gmail

00:14:09.670 --> 00:14:12.610
addresses just so that they
won't show up in a meeting.

00:14:12.610 --> 00:14:15.880
So, being able to be
completely accountless

00:14:15.880 --> 00:14:20.020
and going into a meeting is a
core part of this experience.

00:14:23.290 --> 00:14:25.419
Let's talk more about
mobile, shall we?

00:14:25.419 --> 00:14:26.210
PATRICK WYNN: Sure.

00:14:26.210 --> 00:14:27.710
SERGE LACHAPELLE: I'll
let you take that part

00:14:27.710 --> 00:14:28.940
while I drink a little water.

00:14:28.940 --> 00:14:31.011
PATRICK WYNN: OK.

00:14:31.011 --> 00:14:31.510
Yes.

00:14:31.510 --> 00:14:35.440
So we have mobile clients
for Android and iOS.

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.870
And they are purpose-built for
this enterprise experience,

00:14:37.870 --> 00:14:38.661
as Serge mentioned.

00:14:38.661 --> 00:14:42.100
So, if we switch to Vision.

00:14:42.100 --> 00:14:46.870
So, this is just the
standard Google Pixel.

00:14:46.870 --> 00:14:51.544
And what you'll see here is
that we have an app that's

00:14:51.544 --> 00:14:53.710
designed for this experience,
like you said earlier,

00:14:53.710 --> 00:14:55.189
it has that context built in.

00:14:55.189 --> 00:14:56.730
I don't know if you
noticed we pulled

00:14:56.730 --> 00:14:58.630
the information from Calendar.

00:14:58.630 --> 00:15:01.120
So, if someone has bothered
to put all that prep

00:15:01.120 --> 00:15:03.160
information in the
Calendar, we surface

00:15:03.160 --> 00:15:04.480
that within the meeting.

00:15:04.480 --> 00:15:05.660
And it's no exception here.

00:15:05.660 --> 00:15:07.764
So, I can click on
this meeting here

00:15:07.764 --> 00:15:09.430
and you can see all
the meeting details,

00:15:09.430 --> 00:15:10.930
you can see who's
invited and so on.

00:15:10.930 --> 00:15:13.197
So, it's all
integrated that way.

00:15:13.197 --> 00:15:14.530
And I can also join the meeting.

00:15:14.530 --> 00:15:15.988
So, if I click back
on join, there.

00:15:15.988 --> 00:15:17.870
And it was also
linked there earlier.

00:15:17.870 --> 00:15:20.090
So, we're going to
mute here for a second,

00:15:20.090 --> 00:15:22.180
so we don't get any feedback.

00:15:22.180 --> 00:15:24.575
But this is what the
experience is like for someone.

00:15:24.575 --> 00:15:26.200
So, this is what
Katie sees, basically,

00:15:26.200 --> 00:15:29.210
right now on her iOS device.

00:15:29.210 --> 00:15:30.976
And again, you see
all the same details,

00:15:30.976 --> 00:15:32.600
it's just there to
keep you in context,

00:15:32.600 --> 00:15:35.150
so you can catch
meetings on the fly.

00:15:35.150 --> 00:15:37.240
You don't have to
read your crib notes

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:40.000
and then switch
back to the meeting.

00:15:40.000 --> 00:15:42.430
So, that's all fun.

00:15:42.430 --> 00:15:46.510
I'm going to quit
the meeting for now.

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:50.950
The other thing we realized was
that the reality is that you're

00:15:50.950 --> 00:15:52.990
running on the go to airports.

00:15:52.990 --> 00:15:56.410
I'm sure some of you flew
through airports, or landed

00:15:56.410 --> 00:15:59.530
and took off from them,
at least, to get here.

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:02.950
And when you're on the
go, especially when

00:16:02.950 --> 00:16:05.350
you're roaming or something
like that, the reality--

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:08.020
I know it's 2017, but you
just don't have access

00:16:08.020 --> 00:16:09.626
to the internet on your phone.

00:16:09.626 --> 00:16:11.500
So, you still need to
get into your meetings,

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:12.640
and we understand that.

00:16:12.640 --> 00:16:17.770
So, with every meeting we
have on the enterprise--

00:16:17.770 --> 00:16:20.740
sorry, if you get
Enterprise G Suite,

00:16:20.740 --> 00:16:23.464
every meeting will give you
a dial in number and PIN.

00:16:23.464 --> 00:16:24.880
And that's unique
to that meeting,

00:16:24.880 --> 00:16:28.990
so you don't have to worry
about deploying numbers or host

00:16:28.990 --> 00:16:31.120
PINS and all that
stuff, it just works.

00:16:31.120 --> 00:16:32.960
You just create your
meeting and you get it.

00:16:32.960 --> 00:16:34.750
So, if I join this call here.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:37.360
This is just an
ordinary phone call.

00:16:37.360 --> 00:16:39.520
You can see one
click to connect,

00:16:39.520 --> 00:16:41.580
one to send the meeting code.

00:16:41.580 --> 00:16:44.504
And I'm going to mute again.

00:16:44.504 --> 00:16:45.920
And that just
connects me through,

00:16:45.920 --> 00:16:47.830
so I don't have to
type in the PIN,

00:16:47.830 --> 00:16:50.572
and it'll just help me join
the meeting a lot quicker.

00:16:50.572 --> 00:16:52.030
So, you'll see once
that's entered,

00:16:52.030 --> 00:16:55.760
then I'll join the meeting
and you're on the way.

00:16:55.760 --> 00:16:59.920
Now, the other thing
to show is that--

00:16:59.920 --> 00:17:01.450
I'm going to quit
that for a second.

00:17:04.030 --> 00:17:06.550
We've integrated that directly
into Google Calendar as well.

00:17:06.550 --> 00:17:10.020
So, if I jump back
into the calendar here,

00:17:10.020 --> 00:17:13.450
you can see here's the original
Calendar event you saw earlier.

00:17:13.450 --> 00:17:16.750
All those details are also
in the Calendar event invite.

00:17:16.750 --> 00:17:19.990
So, if I click the phone
number there again,

00:17:19.990 --> 00:17:23.770
you get the same
click-to-join experience.

00:17:23.770 --> 00:17:25.270
So, generally, it's
about two clicks

00:17:25.270 --> 00:17:26.311
and you're into the call.

00:17:26.311 --> 00:17:29.130
Doesn't matter whether
you're on iOS or Android.

00:17:29.130 --> 00:17:30.210
And that's that.

00:17:30.210 --> 00:17:32.210
SERGE LACHAPELLE: Thank you.

00:17:32.210 --> 00:17:34.290
And that's an important
thing to remember.

00:17:34.290 --> 00:17:36.450
Every meeting you
create with this system,

00:17:36.450 --> 00:17:38.850
if you're an Enterprise
G Suite user,

00:17:38.850 --> 00:17:40.980
you will get a phone number
and PIN attached to it

00:17:40.980 --> 00:17:41.640
automatically.

00:17:41.640 --> 00:17:43.220
It's fire and forget.

00:17:43.220 --> 00:17:47.160
That's the default
part of the experience.

00:17:47.160 --> 00:17:49.470
I learned that not roam--

00:17:49.470 --> 00:17:52.350
I roamed too much
internationally last week

00:17:52.350 --> 00:17:55.710
and I lost data access because
I had to confirm to my carrier

00:17:55.710 --> 00:18:00.600
that, yes, I had
spent up all my data.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.090
And then, I wasn't able to
join a meeting, boop, click,

00:18:04.090 --> 00:18:06.040
switched to the mobile phone.

00:18:06.040 --> 00:18:07.764
So, thank you, Patrick.

00:18:07.764 --> 00:18:09.930
PATRICK WYNN: Do you want
to switch back the slides?

00:18:09.930 --> 00:18:11.190
SERGE LACHAPELLE: Let's
switch back to the slides.

00:18:11.190 --> 00:18:11.690
Thank you.

00:18:16.280 --> 00:18:19.550
We've been testing
this since September

00:18:19.550 --> 00:18:24.110
at over 300 companies
that are G Suite users.

00:18:24.110 --> 00:18:27.560
We've had 15,000
end-user accounts

00:18:27.560 --> 00:18:31.760
creating Hangouts
meetings since then.

00:18:31.760 --> 00:18:35.600
And the feedback has been
really positive, so, thank you

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:40.190
to all of us who are part
of our early access program.

00:18:40.190 --> 00:18:44.480
With our UX research group
we've done a lot of interviews,

00:18:44.480 --> 00:18:47.330
we've done a lot of surveys,
and we can confidently

00:18:47.330 --> 00:18:51.710
say that this user group has
felt that audio and video

00:18:51.710 --> 00:18:54.589
quality, their satisfaction
with audio and video quality

00:18:54.589 --> 00:18:55.505
has more than doubled.

00:18:59.250 --> 00:19:04.910
And I think that was an
amazing moment for us

00:19:04.910 --> 00:19:05.990
to get those results in.

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:15.510
So, we're talking about
an enterprise context,

00:19:15.510 --> 00:19:19.500
we're talking about a shift of
video conferencing experience

00:19:19.500 --> 00:19:21.510
into the enterprise.

00:19:21.510 --> 00:19:25.500
And it's very important
for us to show,

00:19:25.500 --> 00:19:29.250
not only that we're
not stopping here,

00:19:29.250 --> 00:19:32.070
we've got a team of people
working on amazing features

00:19:32.070 --> 00:19:34.340
coming up over the
next few months.

00:19:34.340 --> 00:19:37.420
I can see some of my engineering
colleagues here a bit nervous,

00:19:37.420 --> 00:19:42.600
and want me to spill the beans,
but I will spill some beans.

00:19:42.600 --> 00:19:44.820
So, here's what
we're working on,

00:19:44.820 --> 00:19:48.360
and here's what we expect
to be able to either have

00:19:48.360 --> 00:19:53.460
in your hands as testers,
or as a full-blown feature

00:19:53.460 --> 00:19:55.860
before the summer.

00:19:55.860 --> 00:19:59.036
Summer in Sweden comes a little
bit later, so just so you know.

00:19:59.036 --> 00:20:01.470
[LAUGHS]

00:20:01.470 --> 00:20:05.190
Internally at Google, we've
turned this on a few weeks ago.

00:20:05.190 --> 00:20:09.720
You can save meetings
to Google Drive,

00:20:09.720 --> 00:20:13.500
and you can share them as
a video file afterwards.

00:20:13.500 --> 00:20:16.260
And we feel that this is
going to be super impactful,

00:20:16.260 --> 00:20:18.990
and we'll be releasing this
over the next couple of months.

00:20:18.990 --> 00:20:21.810
And we're really excited
about this feature.

00:20:21.810 --> 00:20:25.050
For certain
enterprise customers,

00:20:25.050 --> 00:20:28.230
this is not possible, this
should not be allowed,

00:20:28.230 --> 00:20:32.310
so, of course, you will
have the levers inside

00:20:32.310 --> 00:20:34.770
of your administrator
panel to turn this on

00:20:34.770 --> 00:20:37.605
or off at your desire.

00:20:40.180 --> 00:20:44.110
Hangouts Meet today
supports 30 fully

00:20:44.110 --> 00:20:45.850
participative participants.

00:20:45.850 --> 00:20:49.390
I shouldn't have written
that, it was a tongue twister.

00:20:52.230 --> 00:20:53.910
And this is important.

00:20:53.910 --> 00:20:58.590
This isn't a system where
I pass a speaker baton

00:20:58.590 --> 00:21:01.890
and say, "It's your turn,
now it's your turn to talk".

00:21:01.890 --> 00:21:06.360
This is a system that works for
teams, where everyone can talk,

00:21:06.360 --> 00:21:08.400
where everyone can interact.

00:21:08.400 --> 00:21:12.060
And so, building
a system like that

00:21:12.060 --> 00:21:16.830
means a much higher load
on the infrastructure

00:21:16.830 --> 00:21:18.570
and on the clients.

00:21:18.570 --> 00:21:22.090
But we're happy to announce
that, again, before the summer,

00:21:22.090 --> 00:21:26.940
we will be supporting larger
meeting rooms of up to 50 fully

00:21:26.940 --> 00:21:29.960
participative participants.

00:21:29.960 --> 00:21:30.460
Thank you.

00:21:35.930 --> 00:21:37.850
Everything that
you've seen doesn't

00:21:37.850 --> 00:21:41.360
require any server, any
infrastructure locally,

00:21:41.360 --> 00:21:45.080
it's all Cloud-powered.

00:21:45.080 --> 00:21:51.800
But, as an enterprise,
you are also

00:21:51.800 --> 00:21:55.610
conscious of bandwidth
costs, and so are we.

00:21:55.610 --> 00:21:57.950
So, we've been working
hard at upgrading our Cloud

00:21:57.950 --> 00:22:02.180
infrastructure to reduce the
requirements on bandwidth

00:22:02.180 --> 00:22:04.910
for powering these meetings.

00:22:04.910 --> 00:22:08.180
And so, there's no
on-premise software,

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:10.340
no on-premise hardware.

00:22:10.340 --> 00:22:12.380
Everything is
powered by our Cloud,

00:22:12.380 --> 00:22:15.860
and the requirements for
driving beautiful meetings

00:22:15.860 --> 00:22:18.200
from Hangouts Meet
are going to go down

00:22:18.200 --> 00:22:19.880
over the next few
months, as well.

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:22.390
As we've improved a lot
of our infrastructure.

00:22:22.390 --> 00:22:25.730
And we've also worked
closely with the Chrome team.

00:22:25.730 --> 00:22:29.900
As an administrator, you
can now push a Chrome policy

00:22:29.900 --> 00:22:34.250
that tells the browser to
use this range of UDP ports

00:22:34.250 --> 00:22:39.090
because your corporate firewall
and your internal security

00:22:39.090 --> 00:22:43.530
responsibles might have these
policies already in place.

00:22:43.530 --> 00:22:46.850
So, as an administrator, you
can tell all of your browsers

00:22:46.850 --> 00:22:49.730
to go to this set of ports.

00:22:49.730 --> 00:22:51.890
And that means that
connections will probably

00:22:51.890 --> 00:22:53.680
happen a lot more direct.

00:22:53.680 --> 00:22:56.120
And, in the past, if
ports were blocked

00:22:56.120 --> 00:22:59.050
or the right port
range wasn't used,

00:22:59.050 --> 00:23:03.950
Hangouts might try to
default and tunnel itself

00:23:03.950 --> 00:23:05.930
over web traffic.

00:23:05.930 --> 00:23:07.730
And we all know
web traffic is very

00:23:07.730 --> 00:23:10.970
different to real-time
traffic requirements of audio

00:23:10.970 --> 00:23:11.930
and video.

00:23:11.930 --> 00:23:16.280
So, this is to help improve
your connection rates

00:23:16.280 --> 00:23:19.130
and also help improve the
performance and the quality

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:20.630
that you see.

00:23:20.630 --> 00:23:24.380
We're also going to be
releasing, as part of Chrome,

00:23:24.380 --> 00:23:27.860
support for authenticated
web proxies.

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:30.590
And, yes, this also
feels like VGA,

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:33.740
but there are many, many,
many companies today

00:23:33.740 --> 00:23:39.470
where all the browsers,
all the internet access,

00:23:39.470 --> 00:23:42.740
is done through web
proxies still today.

00:23:42.740 --> 00:23:46.519
And, so having support
for that means that you'll

00:23:46.519 --> 00:23:47.810
be able to talk to your banker.

00:23:51.990 --> 00:23:55.800
I've worked at
Google for 10 years.

00:23:55.800 --> 00:24:01.380
I've never received feedback
like this for a product.

00:24:01.380 --> 00:24:03.940
And these are
customers on our EAP,

00:24:03.940 --> 00:24:07.080
I have grayed out the names
and email addresses, that

00:24:07.080 --> 00:24:12.120
went through the bug
filing tool to tell us

00:24:12.120 --> 00:24:13.620
that they loved the experience.

00:24:13.620 --> 00:24:14.830
That has never happened.

00:24:14.830 --> 00:24:18.900
So, for me, that
was a moment when

00:24:18.900 --> 00:24:21.480
we sat together with the team
and said, "Yes, we should

00:24:21.480 --> 00:24:22.740
go for it and launch it".

00:24:22.740 --> 00:24:26.130
So, thank you to all that
have submitted this feedback.

00:24:26.130 --> 00:24:32.290
It's been an amazing source
of motivation for us.

00:24:32.290 --> 00:24:33.169
Thank you.

00:24:33.169 --> 00:24:35.564
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:38.920 --> 00:24:42.970
So, we're going to continue
to work really, really

00:24:42.970 --> 00:24:46.670
hard at polishing, polishing,
polishing, removing friction

00:24:46.670 --> 00:24:48.790
everywhere we see it.

00:24:48.790 --> 00:24:51.010
And our model is,
really, to give you

00:24:51.010 --> 00:24:56.770
16.7% of your online
meeting work day back,

00:24:56.770 --> 00:25:01.630
so that you have time for these
kinds of Hangouts, instead.

00:25:01.630 --> 00:25:02.770
Thank you very much.

00:25:02.770 --> 00:25:04.570
[APPLAUSE]

00:25:09.370 --> 00:25:12.720
[MUSIC PLAYING]

